from kjvlib import text as T
C = [
("h1", "6. “Eternal”: Matthew 25:46, Daniel 12:2 and Jude 7"),
("p", "Claim: Scripture sets one adjective on both the life of the righteous and the punishment of the wicked, and sets it on the contempt of Daniel 12:2 and on the fire of Jude 7. The adjective can describe a result that lasts, and the volume says so. In these passages, read in their settings, the result that is said to last is one that persons go into, and the passages do not describe it as a thing completed and finished."),
("sub", "6.1 One word on both sides of one verse"),
("p", "The Lord ends His account of the judgment of the nations with a single sentence: “And these shall go away into everlasting punishment: but the righteous into life eternal” (Matthew 25:46). The King James Version renders the one adjective of the original by two English words, “everlasting” and “eternal”, for the sake of the sound. The adjective is the same on both sides of the colon. If the life is without end, the punishment is described by the same word. The reader is not asked to take it in a different sense in the second clause than in the first, and a reading that makes the life endless and the punishment a momentary act does so without a hint from the verse."),
("p", "This is the strongest single support the volume takes from the verse, and it is not a proof taken alone. The writer on the other side answers that the adjective describes the quality or the result of what it qualifies. The life is of the age to come and it lasts, because the persons live it. The punishment is of the age to come and its result lasts, because the persons are gone. The reply is not an evasion. The New Testament speaks of “eternal redemption” (Hebrews 9:12), and the redemption is not a process still going on: it was obtained once, by the blood, and its result is for ever. It speaks of “eternal salvation” (Hebrews 5:9) and of “eternal judgment” (Hebrews 6:2), and in each the adjective states that the result does not lapse. The volume grants that the adjective has this use."),
("p", "It grants it and asks what the verse says the persons do. The righteous go “into life eternal”, and the wicked “shall go away into everlasting punishment”. Both groups proceed to their assigned destinies, and the same adjective qualifies punishment and life. The parallel strongly establishes the permanence of both outcomes, though the verse alone does not explain how the punishment is experienced. The noun for punishment is used in only one other place in the New Testament, in the verse, “There is no fear in love; but perfect love casteth out fear: because fear hath torment” (1 John 4:18), where what it names is a thing felt by one who is afraid. The volume does not make this a lexical proof, and the noun alone does not settle the duration or the manner of the punishment. It notes that the one other place in which the noun stands is one in which the thing named is felt."),
("tbl", "Where an adjective of age or duration is set on a result.", [
    ("Hebrews 9:12", T("Heb 9:12"), "Eternal redemption: the redemption was obtained once and its result does not lapse."),
    ("Hebrews 5:9", T("Heb 5:9"), "Eternal salvation: the salvation is of Christ and is not temporary."),
    ("Hebrews 6:2", T("Heb 6:2"), "Eternal judgment: listed among the first principles; the sentence is not reversed."),
    ("Matthew 25:46", T("Matt 25:46"), "The same adjective on the punishment of the wicked and the life of the righteous; the persons go into each."),
  ], [0.2, 0.45, 0.35]),
("sub", "6.2 The setting of Matthew 25"),
("p", "The verse closes the Olivet Discourse, which the Lord spoke on the Mount of Olives to the disciples who asked Him privately, “what shall be the sign of thy coming, and of the end of the world?” (Matthew 24:3). The scene is the Son of man’s coming “in his glory” (Matthew 25:31), when “before him shall be gathered all nations” (Matthew 25:32) and He divides them as a shepherd divides his sheep from the goats. The ground of the division is what they have done to “one of the least of these my brethren” (Matthew 25:40, 45), and the King’s brethren are the people to whom the discourse was addressed. This is the setting of Israel’s Kingdom, at the King’s return, and it is the setting the volume holds to. The verse is a witness to the fate of the wicked in that judgment and is not applied to the Body of Christ. Who is judged where, and why the Scripture does not merge the courts, is the work of Volume 5 of the Prophecy & Mystery Commentary Series, Volume 3 of the Gospel Commentary Series and Study 31, and it is not repeated here."),
("p", "Within the setting, the King’s words to the goats are the sentence the verse then names: “Depart from me, ye cursed, into everlasting fire, prepared for the devil and his angels” (Matthew 25:41). The fire is everlasting, it is a place into which persons go, and it was prepared for the devil and his angels. The words do not say that the persons in it are tormented or that they are consumed. They say that the fire is everlasting, and that the wicked share the destination prepared for the devil and his angels. The volume holds that what the Revelation says of the devil’s place in the lake of fire is to be read beside this (Revelation 20:10), and it does that in chapter 8, not here."),
("sub", "6.3 Daniel 12:2"),
("p", "The prophet’s word is spoken in the setting of Israel and her last trouble: “at that time shall Michael stand up, the great prince which standeth for the children of thy people: and there shall be a time of trouble, such as never was since there was a nation even to that same time: and at that time thy people shall be delivered, every one that shall be found written in the book” (Daniel 12:1). The next verse is the one the volume reads: “And many of them that sleep in the dust of the earth shall awake, some to everlasting life, and some to shame and everlasting contempt” (Daniel 12:2)."),
("p", "The adjective stands here too, and it stands on both sides again. There is “everlasting life” and there is “everlasting contempt”. Both groups are said to awake. The second group does not awake to be ended; it awakes “to shame and everlasting contempt”. The word for contempt is a word of abhorrence, and it is the same word that the prophet Isaiah uses at the end of his book: “they shall be an abhorring unto all flesh” (Isaiah 66:24). The two are the only places in the Old Testament where the word stands, and the volume reads them together in chapter 7. The point to be made here is that the verse describes a contempt that is everlasting, and a contempt is held by someone toward someone. For the contempt to go on, the persons who are held in it must go on being what it is held against."),
("p", "This is Inferred, not Stated, and the volume says so. A reader who holds that the persons cease can answer that the contempt is the memory in which they are held. That reading is not impossible. The volume holds that the verse says more naturally what it appears to say, that those who awake to shame awake to a lasting shame, and that it is stated in a verse where the same adjective is set on the life of the others. The verse does not bear the whole case, and it does not need to."),
("sub", "6.4 Jude 7"),
("p", "The third passage is the hardest for the volume’s side and the volume takes it so. Jude writes “to them that are sanctified by God the Father, and preserved in Jesus Christ, and called” (Jude 1:1), and he warns of “certain men crept in unawares, who were before of old ordained to this condemnation” (Jude 1:4). He gives three examples of the judgment that follows. The Lord “afterward destroyed them that believed not” (Jude 1:5). The angels who left their own habitation are “reserved in everlasting chains under darkness unto the judgment of the great day” (Jude 1:6). And “Sodom and Gomorrha, and the cities about them in like manner, giving themselves over to fornication, and going after strange flesh, are set forth for an example, suffering the vengeance of eternal fire” (Jude 1:7)."),
("p", "The annihilationist reading of the last example is a strong one. The cities of the plain are not burning today. Peter says that God turned “the cities of Sodom and Gomorrha into ashes”, “making them an ensample unto those that after should live ungodly” (2 Peter 2:6). The fire that destroyed them was God’s, and it is called eternal because its result is permanent: the cities are gone and will not return. On this reading “eternal fire” in Jude 7 is the same kind of statement as “eternal redemption”, a statement about a result, and the cities are the example of what that fire does. The volume grants all of it. If Jude 7 stood alone it would be a verse on which the two sides could differ and neither could say that the verse decides."),
("p", "It does not stand alone. The cities are “set forth for an example”, and an example is an example of something other than itself, of the vengeance of eternal fire that those who follow the same course will suffer. The verse goes on, in the same sentence, to say that the cities are “suffering” it, in the words of the King James Version, and the persons who gave themselves over are the ones named as the subject of the suffering. The preceding verse speaks of angels held “in everlasting chains under darkness”, and a verse later the false teachers are “wandering stars, to whom is reserved the blackness of darkness for ever” (Jude 1:13). A reader who says that Jude’s examples are all of results and not of states has to give an account of chains, darkness reserved, and a suffering. The volume does not say that these decide the question. It says that Jude 7 is one of several places in which the language is more naturally read as describing a condition of the persons than the end of them, and that it takes its place in the cumulative case."),
("ctbl", "The three passages and what each carries.", [
    ("Passage", "What the adjective is set on", "What the volume takes from it"),
    ("Matthew 25:46", "The punishment and the life, by the same adjective", "The parallel is natural; the persons go into each; the adjective may name a result, and the volume does not rest on it alone"),
    ("Daniel 12:2", "The contempt and the life, by the same adjective", "A contempt that is everlasting presupposes persons held in it; this is Inferred"),
    ("Jude 7", "The fire, with the cities set forth as an example", "The conditionalist reading is granted as possible; the example, the chains and the suffering are read as pointing past the ashes"),
  ], [0.2, 0.34, 0.46]),
("sub", "6.5 What the adjective does and does not decide"),
("p", "The chapter began with a claim, and the claim has been kept. The adjective in these three passages can describe a result. It can also describe a state that lasts. No grammar settles which, and the volume does not pretend it does. What settles it, if anything does, is what the passages say the persons do and what is done to them. In Matthew 25 they go away into the punishment and into the fire shared with the devil and his angels. In Daniel 12 they awake to a shame that does not end. In Jude 7 the cities are an example of a vengeance suffered. In each, the persons are the subject of what is said, and the verb is not the verb of ending."),
("p", "The Scripture also says that “the wrath of God abideth” on the one who believes not the Son (John 3:36), and “abideth” is a word for what remains and does not pass. The statement is made of the living and is not a statement about the age to come, and the volume uses it only to say that the New Testament speaks of wrath that remains on persons. The adjectives of this chapter are of the same kind: they speak of persons and of an outcome that does not lapse."),
("sub", "What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "One adjective stands on the punishment and on the life in Matthew 25:46, and the persons go into each; the volume does not read the adjective in two senses.",
    "The adjective can describe a lasting result, as in eternal redemption, eternal salvation and eternal judgment; the volume grants this and rests nothing on the adjective alone.",
    "Matthew 25 is a word to Israel at the King’s return and is a witness to the fate of the wicked, not a word to the Body.",
    "Daniel 12:2 sets an everlasting contempt beside everlasting life, and a contempt that goes on presupposes those who are held in it; this is Inferred.",
    "Jude 7 can be read as an example of a result, and the volume grants it; the chains, the darkness reserved and the suffering of the same sentence point past that reading.",
    "None of the three passages is made to prove continuing experience by itself; each is a part of the cumulative case.",
  ]),
]
